CloudBurrow patch 103 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1823). Applies after patch 0095.

The bug: the catalog of a database is one, shared by its connections, and
holds every spec they saved or deleted from the moment they did. Its Sync,
before each statement is analyzed, reads the catalog table's rows saved
since the last Sync (updatedAt >= lastSyncedAt) on the connection of that
statement. Since bigquery-emulator patch 0102 a read runs beside the
writer, in WAL mode, on a snapshot of the last commit, which may be older
than what the catalog holds: it may hold a row a later save replaced, or a
later delete removed. The rows' times follow the front's clock, which
moves, and are compared as text (RFC3339Nano, its trailing zeros trimmed,
so not in time order within a second), so such an older row could pass the
filter, and Sync put the older spec back over the newer one, or a dropped
table back in the catalog (making it again then failed: it exists).

The patch: the catalog records the names of the rows it writes or deletes
(Catalog.written), and Sync skips a row of such a name. The catalog is the
only writer of its database's rows (the process's one catalog of that
database), so what it holds for such a name is the newest. Rows it did not
write, those of a database it opened, are read as before.

Regression test TestCatalogSyncOnAnOlderSnapshot.

